# Themes

A theme is the set of colours, fonts and corner sizes a site uses. In Howdy
UI it is a plain Gleam record, and every [component](/docs/ui/components)
reads its values from it. Change the theme and every button, card and link
follows, with no component touched.

Two themes are built in, `theme.light()` and `theme.dark()`, and a
[page](/docs/ui/pages) offers both unless told otherwise. Five presets,
`zinc`, `stone`, `rose`, `green` and `violet`, are in `howdy/ui/themes`;
see them on the [components pages](/components/zinc).

To make your own without writing it by hand, use the
[theme maker](/theme-maker). Start from a preset, pick your colours, and
watch real components redraw in them while every colour pair is checked
for contrast. It gives you a light and a dark theme as a Gleam module to
download, with the code that adds it to your app.

## Making a theme

Start from a built-in theme and change what you need. Each part of a theme
has a function that takes an update, so you can change one value and keep
the rest:

```gleam
import howdy/ui/theme.{Colors, Font, Radius}

pub fn brand() -> theme.Theme {
  theme.light()
  |> theme.named("brand")
  |> theme.colors(fn(c) { Colors(..c, primary: "#0f766e", on_primary: "#fff") })
  |> theme.font(fn(f) { Font(..f, heading: "Georgia, serif") })
  |> theme.radius(fn(r) { Radius(..r, medium: "0") })
}
```

`theme.named` gives the theme its name. Names must be unique among the
themes a page offers, so rename a theme you derive from a built-in one if
you offer the original as well.

## What a theme holds

Colours are any CSS colour, as a string:

- `background` is the page itself.
- `surface` is cards, panels and inputs, which sit on the background.
- `border` is the line around surfaces and inputs.
- `text` is body text, and `text_muted` is captions and placeholders.
- `primary` is buttons and links, and `primary_hover` the same when hovered.
- `on_primary` is text placed on top of `primary`.
- `danger` is destructive actions and errors, with `on_danger` for text on
  top of it.
- `focus` is the ring around the focused element.

The `on_` colours exist so that text stays readable. When you change
`primary`, check `on_primary` against it: white text on a pale primary
cannot be read. Aim for a contrast ratio of at least 4.5 to 1.

Fonts are CSS font stacks: `body`, `heading` and `mono`. Radii are CSS
lengths: `small`, `medium` and `large`.

A theme also has a `scheme`, `Light` or `Dark`. It tells the browser which
kind of theme this is, so that scrollbars and built-in form controls match,
and it decides which theme a visitor's system setting selects.

## Offering themes to a page

A page offers a default theme and any number of alternatives:

```gleam
import howdy/ui/page
import howdy/ui/theme

page.new("Orders")
|> page.themes(theme.themes(default: brand(), alternatives: [theme.dark()]))
```

`theme.default_themes()` is the built-in pair, light by default with dark as
the alternative, and is what a page uses when you do not call `page.themes`.
Define your themes once, in a function, and use it for every page.

Which theme a visitor sees is decided in this order:

- If the page names one with `page.theme`, and it is one the page offers,
  that theme is used.
- Otherwise, the visitor's system setting decides. If it asks for the
  opposite scheme to the default's, the first alternative with that scheme
  is used. With the themes above, a visitor whose system is set to dark
  gets `dark`.
- Otherwise the default is used.

## Letting visitors choose

`ui.theme_toggle` is a button that switches between two named themes:

```gleam
import howdy/ui
import lustre/element.{text}

ui.theme_toggle([text("Toggle theme")], from: "brand", to: "dark")
```

Clicking it changes the theme in place, with no reload, and stores the
choice in a cookie called `theme` that lasts a year. Read the cookie when
rendering a page so the choice sticks:

```gleam
import howdy/cookie

fn orders(ctx: Context) {
  use theme <- cookie.string_or(ctx, "theme", default: "system")

  page.new("Orders")
  |> page.themes(my_themes())
  |> page.theme(theme)
  |> page.body([ui.h1("Orders")])
  |> page.respond(ctx)
}
```

`page.theme` ignores a name the page does not offer, so the `"system"`
default, and any nonsense someone puts in the cookie, leaves the choice to
the system setting.

The toggle sets the cookie from the browser, so it is not `HttpOnly`. That
is fine: it holds a colour preference, not a secret. It works the same in a
plain page and inside a [live component](/docs/ui/live-components).

## How it works

Each theme becomes a block of CSS custom properties, all starting
`--howdy-`:

```css
:root { color-scheme: light; --howdy-primary: #0f766e; ... }
[data-theme="brand"] { color-scheme: light; --howdy-primary: #0f766e; ... }
[data-theme="dark"] { color-scheme: dark; --howdy-primary: #60a5fa; ... }
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
  :root:not([data-theme]) { color-scheme: dark; --howdy-primary: #60a5fa; ... }
}
```

The default goes on `:root`. Every theme is also written under a
`data-theme` selector, which is what `page.theme` and the toggle set. The
media query applies the alternative for visitors who have not chosen.

Components never name a colour. They use *tokens* from
`howdy/ui/theme/tokens`, each of which is a reference to one of those
properties, such as `var(--howdy-primary)`. That is why switching theme is
instant and needs no new CSS: the rules stay the same and only the values
of the variables change.

You can use the same properties in your own CSS, and they will follow the
theme too:

```css
.banner { background: var(--howdy-surface); color: var(--howdy-text); }
```

## Keeping themes complete

Because a theme is a record, a theme cannot be half finished. If a new
colour is added to `Colors`, every theme that builds the record in full
fails to compile until it supplies the value. A theme made by updating a
built-in one, as `brand` is above, picks up the built-in value instead.
Either way, no component is ever left reading a colour that a theme forgot
to define.
